<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">God Save the King</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as God Save the Queen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">God Save the King, or God Save the Queen when the monarch is female, is the national anthem of the United Kingdom, first published in the 1740s. It served as the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> until they adopted their own; in Australia it was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advance-australia-fair">Advance Australia Fair</a> in 1984 and retained as the royal anthem.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king.jpg" alt="God Save the King" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:God_Save_the_King_(sheet,_1745).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A separate Australian citizenship was created in 1949, and Advance Australia Fair replaced <b>God Save the Queen</b> as the national anthem in 1984.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The Australian Broadcasting Commission used it to introduce its radio news from 1942 to 1952, and towards the end of the Second World War some cinemas played it alongside &quot;<b>God Save the King</b>&quot; and &quot;The Star-Spangled Banner&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/advance-australia-fair">Advance Australia Fair</a></li><li><blockquote>The Irish Free State at first adopted no anthem: ex-unionists still regarded &quot;<b>God Save the King</b>&quot; as theirs, and W. T. Cosgrave's government feared both deepening unionist hostility in Northern Ireland and leaving the song to its republican opponents.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amhran-na-bhfiann">Amhrán na bhFiann</a></li><li><blockquote>King George V is said to have preferred it to &quot;<b>God Save the King</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/and-did-those-feet-in-ancient-time">Jerusalem (And did those feet in ancient time)</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody is Joseph Haydn's, written in 1797 for Lorenz Leopold Haschka's &quot;Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser&quot;, a birthday anthem for Emperor Francis II modelled on Britain's &quot;<b>God Save the King</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1920s the South African Broadcasting Corporation played it at the close of each day's broadcasts, together with &quot;<b>God Save the King</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-stem-van-suid-afrika">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</a></li></ul></section>
